    "title": "Detailed history of state during Reconstruction offers revelations, failures",
    "text": "ENTERTAINMENT\nDetailed history of state during Reconstruction offers revelations, failures\nJeffrey Boutwell\nSpecial to the Mississippi Clarion Ledger\nFrom 1861 to 1876, three historic meetings took place in the Mississippi State Capitol in Jackson that shaped not only Mississippi’s role during the Civil War and the ensuing Reconstruction period, but also that of American race relations down to the present day.\nIn January 1861, white delegates voted 83-15 in favor of secession and of following South Carolina into the newly formed Confederate States of America, thereby rupturing the Union created by the founding fathers in 1787. They did so, openly declaring that “our position is thoroughly identified with the institution of slavery — the greatest material interest of the world.”",
